$ --help
exit 0
--- stdout
usage: kenaz <command> [flags]

  approvals List write requests and open the approval page
  daemon    Run the on-demand relay (started by mcp and hooks)
  device    Show or rename this device's display name
  doctor    Diagnose installation and configuration
  hook      Harness hook entry point (SessionStart/SessionEnd)
  init      Set everything up: PATH, sign-in, and every app found
  licenses  Print license and notice texts
  login     Sign in to Kameas Fleet
  logout    Sign out and purge local queued data
  mcp       Run the MCP stdio server (started by the harness)
  memory    Manage memories (on this device; optional sync to your Kameas Fleet account)
  migrate   Move files from an earlier release to ~/.kenaz/plugin (close your AI apps first)
  optin     Choose which usage data to share (shows the notice)
  setup     Configure one app (Claude Code or Codex) to use Kameas Fleet
  skills    List or sync shared Agent Skills from Kameas Fleet
  status    Show sign-in, telemetry and consent state (--json)
  version   Print version and build flavor
--- stderr
$ --version
exit 0
--- stdout
kenaz 0.0.0-dev (unofficial build)
--- stderr
$ setup print-managed claude
exit 64
--- stdout
--- stderr
kenaz setup: print-managed pins the plugin to a signed release, and this build has no release commit (it is not an official release); pass --release vX.Y.Z --commit <full commit of that tag>
$ setup print-managed claude --release v1.2.3 --commit 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567
exit 0
--- stdout
# Claude Code managed settings for Kenaz for Claude Code and Codex
#
# Deploy as managed-settings.json:
#   macOS: /Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json
#   Linux: /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json
#   or paste into server-managed settings in the Claude admin console.
# Merge with any managed settings you already deploy. This installs the
# kenaz plugin for every user (they cannot disable it) and routes
# telemetry to the local relay on 127.0.0.1:4318.
#
# Notes:
# - Locking the OTLP destination against user overrides needs Claude Code
#   2.1.217 or later.
# - Claude Desktop sessions take environment variables from the app's
#   launch environment, which wins over settings "env".
# - If port 4318 is taken on some machines, the relay falls back to another
#   port and "kenaz doctor" reports the mismatch.
# - The plugin is pinned to release v1.2.3, commit 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567,
#   with autoUpdate off. Deploy the snippet of each new release to upgrade;
#   do not register the GitHub marketplace without a pin, which follows the
#   repository's default branch.
#
# Each user also needs the kenaz binary on their PATH (the plugin runs it
# directly; install it with your package manager of choice or your MDM; see the
# Kameas Fleet admin guide) and must sign in once with "kenaz login".
# Admin configuration never signs anyone in or turns on telemetry classes for them:
# nothing is sent until each member signs in and opts in.

----- managed-settings.json -----
{
  "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
    "kenaz": {
      "autoUpdate": false,
      "source": {
        "name": "kenaz",
        "plugins": [
          {
            "description": "Kenaz, a Kameas AI product. Connects AI tools such as Claude Code and Codex to your organization's Kameas Fleet: shared skills, team context, your private memory and, where you choose, usage summaries. Requires the kenaz program and a Kameas Fleet account. Claude, Claude Code and Cowork are trademarks of Anthropic, PBC. OpenAI and Codex are trademarks of OpenAI. Kenaz and Kameas are trademarks of Kameas AI, Inc. Kenaz is an independent product of Kameas AI, Inc. and is not affiliated with, endorsed or sponsored by Anthropic or OpenAI.",
            "name": "kenaz",
            "source": {
              "path": "plugin",
              "ref": "v1.2.3",
              "sha": "0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567",
              "source": "git-subdir",
              "url": "https://github.com/kameas-ai/kenaz-plugin.git"
            }
          }
        ],
        "source": "settings"
      }
    }
  },
  "enabledPlugins": {
    "kenaz@kenaz": true
  },
  "env": {
    "CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY": "1",
    "OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER": "otlp",
    "OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER": "otlp",
    "OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL": "http/protobuf",
    "OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT": "http://127.0.0.1:4318",
    "OTEL_LOG_USER_PROMPTS": "0",
    "OTEL_LOG_ASSISTANT_RESPONSES": "0",
    "OTEL_LOG_TOOL_DETAILS": "0",
    "OTEL_LOG_TOOL_CONTENT": "0",
    "OTEL_LOG_RAW_API_BODIES": "0"
  }
}
--- stderr
$ setup print-managed codex --release v1.2.3 --commit 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567
exit 0
--- stdout
# Codex managed configuration for Kenaz for Claude Code and Codex
#
# Deploy the block below in managed_config.toml, which overrides each
# user's own [otel] settings:
#   macOS/Linux file: /etc/codex/managed_config.toml
#   macOS MDM: preference domain com.openai.codex, key config_toml_base64
#              (base64 of the block)
# Merge with any [otel] settings you already manage.
#
# To install the plugin for everyone, register the marketplace pinned to
# release v1.2.3 (commit 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567) in the system config.toml
# (/etc/codex/config.toml) or cloud-managed configuration, with the block
# after the managed_config.toml one, and enable kenaz@kenaz for your users.
# Codex checks out "ref" after cloning. That is not tamper-proof: Codex
# runs "git checkout <ref>" without verifying the result, and git prefers a
# branch named like the commit, so whoever controls the repository could
# serve other content. Run "kenaz setup doctor" on a sample of machines: it
# checks that Codex's checkout is the release commit. Do not use an
# unpinned import of kameas-ai/kenaz-plugin: it follows the repository's default
# branch. Plugin hooks still need each user to trust them once in Codex;
# telemetry works without them.
#
# Each user also needs the kenaz binary on their PATH (the plugin runs it
# directly; install it with your package manager of choice or your MDM; see the
# Kameas Fleet admin guide) and must sign in once with "kenaz login".
# Admin configuration never signs anyone in or turns on telemetry classes for them:
# nothing is sent until each member signs in and opts in.

----- managed_config.toml -----
[otel]
exporter = { otlp-http = { endpoint = "http://127.0.0.1:4318/v1/logs", protocol = "binary" } }
trace_exporter = { otlp-http = { endpoint = "http://127.0.0.1:4318/v1/traces", protocol = "binary" } }
metrics_exporter = { otlp-http = { endpoint = "http://127.0.0.1:4318/v1/metrics", protocol = "binary" } }
log_user_prompt = false
tool_result = { max_bytes = 0 }

----- config.toml (marketplace, pinned) -----
[marketplaces.kenaz]
source_type = "git"
source = "https://github.com/kameas-ai/kenaz-plugin.git"
ref = "0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567"
--- stderr
$ setup --help
exit 64
--- stdout
--- stderr
usage:
  kenaz setup claude|codex [--yes] [--dry-run] [--traces] [--env E] [--bundle DIR | --no-bundle]
  kenaz setup --undo claude|codex [--yes] [--dry-run] [--env E]
  kenaz setup print-managed claude|codex [--port N] [--raw] [--release vX.Y.Z --commit SHA]
  kenaz setup doctor [--env E]

To set up PATH, sign-in and every app at once, run kenaz init.

Plugin source (Claude Code, Codex): a downloaded kenaz registers its own
verified plugin by absolute path. A "kenaz" marketplace from GitHub
(kameas-ai/kenaz-plugin) is used instead only when it is pinned to this release's
commit; one that follows a branch or tag is replaced, with a warning.
--bundle DIR registers that bundle's plugin and replaces the GitHub
marketplace; --no-bundle never registers a bundle.
Setup starts the Kenaz relay if it is not running.
